Please describe the bug 🐞
when querying partition information of a day(timestamp_field) partition table.  the spec states day transfrom returns an integer.
when looking at spec.javaClass() it is also integer, but when the transform processes the result type. it seems it puts Date there on purpose like this
  @Override
  public Type getResultType(Type sourceType) {
    if (granularity == ChronoUnit.DAYS) {
      return Types.DateType.get();
    }
    return Types.IntegerType.get();
  }

no idea why would day() transform yield a date class,  while the spec states it should return integer?

Feature Request / Improvement
If the hadoop-common library is not present, trying to write a Parquet file:
DataWriter<Record> dataWriter =
        Parquet.writeData(file)
                .schema(schema)
                .createWriterFunc(GenericParquetWriter::buildWriter)
                .overwrite()
                .withSpec(partitionSpec)
                .build();
... will fail with:
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: org/apache/hadoop/conf/Configuration
        at org.apache.iceberg.parquet.Parquet$WriteBuilder.<init>(Parquet.java:164)
        at org.apache.iceberg.parquet.Parquet$WriteBuilder.<init>(Parquet.java:143)
        at org.apache.iceberg.parquet.Parquet.write(Parquet.java:129)
        at org.apache.iceberg.parquet.Parquet$DataWriteBuilder.<init>(Parquet.java:646)
        at org.apache.iceberg.parquet.Parquet$DataWriteBuilder.<init>(Parquet.java:637)
        at org.apache.iceberg.parquet.Parquet.writeData(Parquet.java:623)

In org.apache.iceberg.parquet.Parquet, an empty Configuration is created:
    private WriteBuilder(OutputFile file) {
      this.file = file;
      if (file instanceof HadoopOutputFile) {
        this.conf = new Configuration(((HadoopOutputFile) file).getConf());
      } else {
        this.conf = new Configuration();
      }
    }
ParquetWriter eventually passes this to ParquetIO.file(), which ignores it if the file is not a HadoopOutputFile.
hadoop-common is a heavy dependency with many transitive dependencies, would be nice to avoid it.
Similar to Iceberg Flink issues - #3117 / #4183
